Task: Wire OrderEventPublisher into StockEventConsumer + Main (US-5.3, Phase 3)

Summary: StockEventConsumer.reservedStream now publishes order.reserved via
OrderEventPublisher once updateStatusByItemId confirms a successful
Pending -> Reserved transition, using the UpdatedOrderRef fields from Phase 1.
Publish is skipped entirely on the no-op case (unknown item id, already
resolved). reservationFailedStream is untouched - order.reserved is never
published from it (confirmed by a dedicated test; the matching
order.status-changed publish for that path is US-5.4, a separate track).
OrderEventPublisher wired as a Resource in Main.scala, nested around
StockEventConsumer's existing background fiber.

Also fixed a test bug while writing the negative-assertion test: the first
version of "order.reserved is never published from reservationFailedStream"
used a bare "no message within N seconds" check against the shared
TestContainerForAll Kafka container, which fails spuriously once an earlier
test in the same suite has already published a (different) order.reserved
message - a stale message from another test's topic state, not a real bug.
Rewrote the helper to check "no message matching *this test's* order id"
instead, which is immune to other tests' leftover messages.

Files changed:
- src/main/scala/orderservice/StockEventConsumer.scala (reservedStream
  publishes on success; new publishReservedIfUpdated helper; run gains a
  publisher parameter)
- src/main/scala/orderservice/Main.scala (wires OrderEventPublisher.resource
  as a new nested Resource)
- src/test/scala/orderservice/StockEventConsumerSuite.scala (4 existing
  tests updated for run's new parameter; 2 new tests: publish-on-success
  with field assertions, and the never-publish-on-failure negative test)

Tests: 111 passed, 0 failed (full suite).

Why: this is the actual point of US-5.3 - proving the full path from a
consumed inventory.stock-reserved event to a published order.reserved event,
end to end, not just the two pieces built in isolation in Phases 1-2.
